      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant",
      "problem": "Let $a=⟨1,-1,2⟩$, $b=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $c=⟨2,4,2⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3)+2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-12-2+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
